Done — `wp-content/themes/riverside/functions.php:10-19` now sets the sender via the `wp_mail_from` and `wp_mail_from_name` filters.

Two things worth flagging:

**I did not edit core.** The hardcoded `wordpress@example.com` / `WordPress` live in `wp-includes/pluggable.php:7-8`. `PROJECT.md` states that platform/framework core changes go only through the update mechanism, never by hand, so I used the documented filters from `wp-content` instead.

**This won't take effect against the copy of core in this repo.** The abridged `wp_mail()` stub in `wp-includes/pluggable.php` assigns `$from_email` and `$from_name` directly and never calls `apply_filters()`, so there is no hook for my filters to attach to. Real WordPress core has applied `wp_mail_from` and `wp_mail_from_name` inside `wp_mail()` since 2.2, and the change is written against that contract — so it will work on an actual WordPress install, and against this tree once core is a real checkout. I couldn't fix that from `wp-content`; making it work against the stub as-is would require hand-editing core, which is the thing PROJECT.md rules out. If this stub reflects the deployed site rather than a placeholder, tell me and we'll take it up as a core update.

I placed the filters in the theme because it's the extension point that already exists and loads without an activation step. If you'd rather the sender identity survive a theme switch, a small mu-plugin is the better home — say the word and I'll move it.
